  "slug": "related-disorder-models-ad7b",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and papers add humanized, inhibitory-neuron and organoid models of SynGAP1, CASK, dynein and TTC5 disorders.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is genetically defined model systems that link specific gene perturbations to neuronal and neurodevelopmental phenotypes. SYNGAP1 c.427C>T knock-in mice, patient iPSC neurons and organoids show reduced SynGAP1 with altered Ras/ERK signaling. CASK deletion in parvalbumin or somatostatin inhibitory neurons produces distinct seizure types tied to different inhibitory synaptic deficits. Patient-derived cortical organoids carrying dynein variants reveal spindle disruption and apoptosis, while Ttc5 knockout mice display brain malformations arising from neurogenesis and mitotic defects, with elevated γ-tubulin. The Drosophila ANKLE2-VRK1 study addresses a related but distinct question: maintenance of proteostasis and neuronal resilience in adult neurons during aging. What is new is the cell-type-resolved or human-derived resolution of several disorder models, and the mechanistic link of TTC5 to γ-tubulin mRNA regulation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
